TASK: Replace Face ID login concept with a secure iOS Face ID App Lock.

IMPORTANT:
BlueMind already uses persistent login/session restore.

Therefore Face ID should NOT be treated as another login provider like Google or Apple.

Remove/avoid any “Continue with Face ID” login-provider architecture.

Face ID should instead protect access to the already authenticated BlueMind session.

====================================================
CORE BEHAVIOR
====================================================

The user logs into BlueMind normally ONE TIME using:

- Email + Password
- Google
- Apple

The authenticated session is securely persisted.

Closing the app must NOT log the user out.

If Face ID Lock is OFF:
→ reopen BlueMind normally using the existing persistent session.

If Face ID Lock is ON:
→ reopen BlueMind
→ immediately show native iOS Face ID
→ only after successful Face ID reveal the authenticated BlueMind interface.

====================================================
1. SETTINGS
====================================================

Add a setting inside:

Settings → Security / Privacy

Name:

Face ID Lock

State:
On / Off

Use native iOS behavior.

Do not create a fake Face ID interface.

====================================================
2. ENABLING FACE ID
====================================================

When the user turns Face ID Lock ON:

1. Verify that the user is currently authenticated.
2. Present the real native iOS Face ID prompt using LocalAuthentication.
3. If Face ID succeeds:
   - save Face ID Lock = enabled
   - protect the relevant secure session access using iOS Keychain/biometric access where appropriate
4. If Face ID fails/cancels:
   - do not enable the setting
   - keep Face ID Lock OFF

Do not ask for the user’s password again just to enable Face ID unless the existing security architecture explicitly requires re-authentication.

====================================================
3. APP LAUNCH
====================================================

When BlueMind launches:

FIRST:
restore/check the existing authenticated session.

Then:

IF no valid session:
→ show normal Get Started/Login.

IF valid session AND Face ID Lock is OFF:
→ enter BlueMind normally.

IF valid session AND Face ID Lock is ON:
→ do NOT reveal private BlueMind content yet.
→ present native iOS Face ID.
→ only unlock the app after Face ID succeeds.

====================================================
4. PRIVACY SCREEN
====================================================

This is important.

While Face ID authentication is pending:

Do NOT briefly show:
- chats
- reminders
- schedules
- AI plans
- settings
- profile information
- user data

Use a neutral BlueMind lock/loading screen if needed.

Private content must remain hidden until authentication succeeds.

Also protect app-switcher snapshots if practical so sensitive content is not exposed while the app is locked/backgrounded.

====================================================
5. FACE ID SUCCESS
====================================================

If Face ID succeeds:

- unlock the existing session
- reveal the app
- do NOT create a new login session
- do NOT ask for Email/Password
- do NOT navigate through Get Started

The user is already logged in.

Face ID only unlocks access to that session.

====================================================
6. FACE ID FAILURE / CANCEL
====================================================

If Face ID:
- fails
- is cancelled
- becomes unavailable

keep BlueMind locked.

Offer appropriate actions such as:

- Try Face ID Again
- Use normal login / Re-authenticate

Do not expose private content after failure.

====================================================
7. MANUAL LOGOUT
====================================================

This is critical.

When the user explicitly presses Log out:

- clear/revoke the authenticated session
- clear refresh/access tokens according to the existing logout system
- clear biometric-protected session material
- disable/remove Face ID access for that old session

After manual logout:

Face ID must NOT reopen the previous account.

The next app launch must show Get Started/Login.

The user must log in normally again.

After a successful new login, they may enable Face ID Lock again.

====================================================
8. SESSION EXPIRATION
====================================================

Face ID must NEVER bypass backend authentication.

If Face ID succeeds but the BlueMind session is:

- expired
- revoked
- invalid
- deleted
- no longer refreshable

then require normal authentication.

Face ID verifies the device user.
It does NOT override session validity.

====================================================
9. NO PASSWORD STORAGE
====================================================

Never store:

- raw password
- plaintext password
- Face ID image
- biometric template
- biometric data

Use Apple LocalAuthentication.

iOS performs biometric matching.

BlueMind should only receive the success/failure result.

====================================================
10. KEYCHAIN
====================================================

Use the iOS Keychain for sensitive persistent authentication material.

Do not rely on plain localStorage for sensitive auth tokens.

Audit the current persistent-login implementation and make sure the session storage and Face ID lock do not conflict.

There should be ONE clean flow:

Backend session
↓
Secure persistent storage
↓
Optional Face ID Lock
↓
App access

====================================================
11. APP BACKGROUND / RESUME
====================================================

Define a predictable locking policy.

At minimum:

- cold launch → require Face ID when enabled
- app terminated and reopened → require Face ID
- device restart → require Face ID
- app returning after an appropriate background duration → lock again

Do not repeatedly trigger Face ID for tiny interruptions such as a system popup unless necessary.

Use a reasonable native app-lock lifecycle.

====================================================
12. FACE ID DISABLED OR CHANGED
====================================================

Handle safely if:

- Face ID is disabled in iOS Settings
- biometrics change
- device passcode changes
- biometric-protected Keychain item becomes invalid
- Face ID hardware is unavailable

Do not crash.
Do not create an authentication loop.

Fall back to normal login/re-authentication safely.

====================================================
13. DO NOT ADD FACE ID TO GET STARTED
====================================================

Do NOT show:

“Continue with Face ID”

next to Google/Apple.

Persistent login already solves repeated account login.

Face ID belongs in Security/Privacy as an optional APP LOCK.

====================================================
14. DO NOT BREAK EXISTING PERSISTENT LOGIN
====================================================

Verify:

Face ID Lock OFF:
Login once
→ close app
→ reopen
→ still logged in.

Face ID Lock ON:
Login once
→ close app
→ reopen
→ Face ID
→ unlock existing session.

Manual Logout:
→ close/reopen
→ Login required.

